# Part Six — The Plan

> A plan turns a syllabus into a timetable. This one takes a complete beginner from the first page to the exam hall in sixteen weeks, alongside other papers.

## 6.1  The sixteen-week timetable

The plan assumes about ==90 minutes a day, six days a week==, for Gender Studies, alongside your other subjects. Order matters: T1 gives you the language of the subject, T2 its theories, and everything after that applies them. The heaviest topics get the most time.

::: table | Sixteen weeks from zero to the exam.
@widths 1.6,4,8.4
Weeks | Study | Output by the end
1 | This Master Anatomy: Part Zero slowly; Parts One–Three quickly | You can define the twelve ideas of §0.3 and explain the seven topics
2–3 | T1KB (the discipline and social construction) | Ten T1 terms defined from memory; two T1QA answers read and re-planned
4–5 | T2KB (feminist theories) | A one-page table of the schools from memory; one practice answer written
6–7 | T3KB (movements) | The waves and the Pakistani phases drawn as timelines; one practice answer
8–10 | T4KB (gender and development) — three weeks, the heaviest topic | WID/WAD/GAD table and the theory comparison from memory; two practice answers
11 | T5KB (status of women in Pakistan) | A one-page data sheet from the Fact Book; one practice answer
12 | T6KB (governance) | The quota history timeline; one practice answer
13 | T7KB (violence and the case studies) | The laws list and the three case studies from memory; one practice answer
14 | All seven TnQA documents: re-plan every past question as an answer map | 84 answer maps (two or three minutes each)
15 | Prediction Papers Sets 1 and 2 under timed conditions; OL bank | Two full papers written and self-marked with §1.5
16 | Revision Notes; Fact Book; Prediction Paper Set 3; MCQ drills | The exam-day plan rehearsed
:::

## 6.2  The daily routine

::: table | A ninety-minute study session.
@widths 2.5,11.5
Minutes | What you do
10 | Revise yesterday: close the book and write the key terms and one diagram from memory.
50 | Read today's section of the Knowledge Base. Stop at every card and say aloud what it proves.
15 | Plan one past question from the topic as an answer map — without looking at the model.
15 | Compare with the model's map; note what you missed. Add three facts to your personal list.
:::

Once a week, write one full answer in thirty minutes by hand. Writing is a separate skill from knowing; it improves only with practice.

## 6.3  The memory routine — using the Fact Book

The subject has perhaps five hundred facts worth knowing: dates, numbers, names, laws and books. Do not try to learn them all at once.

1. **Learn the facts inside their arguments.** A date remembered as part of a story (WAF formed in 1981 *because* of the Hudood Ordinances of 1979) stays; a date in a list fades.
2. **Use the Fact Book by topic, a page a day,** from the week you finish each Knowledge Base.
3. **Use spaced repetition.** Review a page after one day, three days, a week, and a month.
4. **Keep a "numbers card"** — one card with the fifteen statistics you will use most (literacy, labour participation, maternal mortality, the gender-gap rank, women's seats, the electoral gender gap). Update it from the Fact Book before the exam.

## 6.4  The objective part

- **Read the one-liner bank** at the end of each Knowledge Base on the day you finish it.
- **Do all 140 past MCQs** in the OL volume once in week 14 and again in week 16.
- **Concentrate on T1 and T2 names and terms** — they supply nearly two in five MCQs.
- **Keep the laws and dates list** (from the Fact Book) on your wall.

## 6.5  The last fortnight

1. Read Part Three of this document again. Decide your Tier 1 list.
2. Re-read the **Revision Notes** twice, a topic a day.
3. Write the three **Prediction Papers** under exam conditions if you have not already.
4. Re-draw from memory the ten diagrams you will most likely use: the sex/gender comparison, the schools of feminism, the waves timeline, the Pakistani movement timeline, WID → WAD → GAD, the development theories table, the quota history, Galtung's triangle, the ecological model, the cycle of violence.
5. Update your **numbers card** from the Fact Book.

## 6.6  The day of the paper

::: table | The day of the paper, minute by minute.
@widths 3,11
Time | What you do
The night before | Numbers card; the ten diagrams; sleep. Do not start anything new.
Before entering | Pens (blue or black), pencil, ruler, eraser, admit card, CNIC, water.
Minutes 0–25 | Part I: all twenty MCQs. Mark doubtful items; return to them; leave nothing blank.
Minutes 25–35 | Read all seven questions twice. Choose four. Start with your strongest.
Each answer (~33 min) | 3 minutes map; 28 minutes write; 2 minutes check.
Last 8 minutes | Numbers and headings check; underline key terms; make sure all four answers are complete.
:::

::: eye | If a question surprises you
If a question uses a term you do not recognise, look at its other words: most "new" questions are old families in new clothing. 2026 Q6 (NGO-isation) is the Pakistani movement plus the WAD critique; 2026 Q4 (the gender gap report) is the status of women with a new frame. Map it on to what you know, define the new term as best you can from its context, and answer the limbs you can answer fully.
:::
